      "created_at": "2018-10-12T12:00:59Z",
      "updated_at": "2018-10-14T13:54:32Z",
      "body": "The original module added would be no good for the GDPR anyway (probably OK for the soon to be replaced ePrivacy Directive) . If you really need to ask for consent for processing personal data (which may or may not involve a cookie being set), it comes nowhere near the standard required, as you need to record consent.\r\n\r\nSee the \"How should we record consent?\" section at the link below.\r\nhttps://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/guide-to-the-general-data-protection-regulation-gdpr/consent/how-should-we-obtain-record-and-manage-consent/#how4\r\n\r\nFortunately you shouldn't need to use consent for the most part of an online store. Script and cookie wise, the AddThis script on the product page is the probably the main concern, as they collect data from visitors to your site. Ideally you would need to inform, gain consent and record consent before allowing the script to run. The Google reCAPTCHA module may also need consent, if another legal basis can't be justified.",
      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "pin": null,
